#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445103 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc175313-c1cd-5ef5-81ac-641814b985cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=0.8x-0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-0.3y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.25,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3850fcb7-fc55-55b2-8f9f-750fe16ba10c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445104 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.8x-0.4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.3y+1.2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.8x-0.4xy)=0.8-0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.8x-0.4xy)=-0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=1.2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=-0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4y & -0.4x \\\\ 1.2y & -0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4(2) & -0.4(0.25) \\\\ 1.2(2) & -0.3+1.2(0.25)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2.4\\cdot (-0.1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=7x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54015625-9f74-5e9e-ac7d-555ab045f6d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445105 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy & =0 \\\\ x(7-4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{7}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{7}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ -6+3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4.5y-1.5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c2a30e4-7147-5605-9ca8-d3892b9005ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445106 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-2.5x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $3xy$ term)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+6xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8df5273a-07e4-5ba8-bfd6-b4b9ed059597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445107 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-4x+6xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=7y-3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+6xy)=-4+6y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+6xy)=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-3xy)=-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7y-3xy)=7-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-4+6y & 6x \\\\ -3y & 7-3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-4+6(0) & 6(0) \\\\ -3(0) & 7-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=7.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67cf9112644945b23280f4593ffbd70161651bd9cb389293821f022599ea4a75.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac25cc32-c574-57e4-8147-410ae2ef0848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445108 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x-0.9xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6c858ce-3897-5421-9543-5d24a7c86dfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445109 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-3.2y$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2.4xy$ term)." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
